      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Demosaicing playground: Bayer CFA -> RGB, three algorithms scored\n\nA digital sensor is monochrome behind a colour-filter mosaic - each pixel samples only ONE of R/G/B. Demosaicing interpolates the two missing channels back. There is no licence-safe REAL Bayer sample (real RAW is murky 16-bit .pgm), so this does what every reference lab does: MOSAIC a ground-truth RGB into a synthetic CFA, then demosaic it back and MEASURE the reconstruction against the original - which a real Bayer frame could never do, having no ground truth.\n\nPIPELINE: the photo -> 'CV Mosaic (RGB -> Bayer CFA)' drops 2 of 3 channels per pixel in an RGGB pattern (R at (0,0)). The grayscale CFA preview shows the 2x2 sampling. Three cv2.demosaicing codes then reconstruct it: BILINEAR (fast, averages neighbours - visible zipper on edges), VNG (variable number of gradients - picks the smoothest direction), EA (edge-aware - follows edges to suppress colour fringing). Each row shows the result, its PSNR vs the original (higher dB = better), and an absdiff heatmap (the colour-scale bar reads the peak per-channel error - the zipper/moire the metric penalises).\n\ngingham_check (a regular green/white check weave) is deliberately a worst case: fine periodic detail is exactly what aliases into colour moire, so the three algorithms visibly separate."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
